 =7\\cdot 0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(7,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-17137/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-17137"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-276791/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "057b6b8e-7efa-5e4a-9f17-42680386a2ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276791/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9fd9d0c0-f207-55c3-be9d-9b5a92b66369" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-276791/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-276791/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-276791/field-2/127f06aafda48787b3a8c9b5e8b9215260a70c483da41a5be92a3de54e74a3f4" :answer/id #uuid "3c6418e2-73ac-5e70-89f7-cc821a004a8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276791/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "f64753f3-c12f-53f3-a5a1-c80e9f76c8c7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-276791/field-2/127f06aafda48787b3a8c9b5e8b9215260a70c483da41a5be92a3de54e74a3f4" :answer-field/choices ["q-276791/field-2/127f06aafda48787b3a8c9b5e8b9215260a70c483da41a5be92a3de54e74a3f4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-276791" :question/id #uuid "91859d58-dbae-5830-8515-ca5a18c13524" :question/math-academy-id "q-276791" :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $12,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${60}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=12,\\,\\theta ={60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(6,6\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-276791/field-1" "q-276791/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-276791"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-17134/selection/c67ef98b3da67da9bc9f420278edfa7c175eb4f05496e2a70aa87d2f9d214f1f" :answer/id #uuid "c1b67b96-8fc7-5580-b625-6fbc1c3d1c60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-17134/selection/d8605f1c44eab550ebe99b1259bbc6e0c47d327797573e0220d31e966a1bab76" :answer/id #uuid "12faf59b-0877-575e-89b5-871b697d27e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-17134/selection/bd38bfeb9bfc673ffe6d76eaface75e9af2f65fcceb3ce6b0329c0c6d5123d6a" :answer/id #uuid "e0bf1565-973f-5ec9-af0a-b3a28afc975a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-17134/selection/503f4e8720f816f9121f32a3a66b5216375bc52ff6e165675bab8b4b430363b0" :answer/id #uuid "709161f6-4301-5a3