 $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\ y & =4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2,-2\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-17134/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-17134"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120328/selection/3f9ec0d832a51f23b50e8a055ec9f58f037a6db332b812e55ecfafcbd321497b" :answer/id #uuid "c26a53a0-91c4-52d4-baab-d09ef8fabb4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120328/selection/0972cb22bacfffae981dbf80a454508b2ef7efb2de81dac70f862e51a29bcab2" :answer/id #uuid "597879b4-34e1-5300-acf7-fc9ab54b42ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120328/selection/ca0aa61cbdfd19b69677c8e049dd19aff3d0f8eb12e589882b46e4f5d029f4ec" :answer/id #uuid "438f43d4-5f45-5c1a-b6af-5f42ad3c058b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-6\\sqrt{2},6\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120328/selection/7dff0200be85a4d5a1472bc14c04e7408fa5b8f4471a66bcaef618059fe4d738" :answer/id #uuid "9926c3e4-724d-5bf3-b4f6-ba23068c972c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120328/selection/c794bd91fafb22a9d44d418b426dc48e1f5ff8baeb0fa9196ef9f62ad2b94178" :answer/id #uuid "723bc5ed-fa57-5534-be9f-fb42a5237071" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120328/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3eb2da5f-b177-521a-89fc-c3bd4625394a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120328/selection/c794bd91fafb22a9d44d418b426dc48e1f5ff8baeb0fa9196ef9f62ad2b94178" :answer-field/choices ["q-120328/selection/3f9ec0d832a51f23b50e8a055ec9f58f037a6db332b812e55ecfafcbd321497b" "q-120328/selection/0972cb22bacfffae981dbf80a454508b2ef7efb2de81dac70f862e51a29bcab2" "q-120328/selection/ca0aa61cbdfd19b69677c8e049dd19aff3d0f8eb12e589882b46e4f5d029f4ec" "q-120328/selection/7dff0200be85a4d5a1472bc14c04e7408fa5b8f4471a66bcaef618059fe4d738" "q-120328/selection/c794bd91fafb22a9d44d418b426dc48e1f5ff8baeb0fa9196ef9f62ad2b94178"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120328" :question/id #uuid "dd295ef1-565e-5082-b9b4-b1e2a3d77e84" :question/math-academy-id "q-120328" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $6,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${315}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=6,\\,\\theta ={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120328/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120328"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-86619/selection/9b81f8a8be5c85a0e797ee8baaeba21f0dc31b57428fc18c3dd70922899b39be" :answer/id #uuid "6ecf2784-822a-58b8-b2ba-f7e819ffd3c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{3},3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86619/selection/ec482aa227fa390257d545d04f1877e92823b4d16cc98918069db0c4b0f1da14" :answer/id #uuid "24cc3c30-cbee-5b85-bb53-cbe94ef9511f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86619/selection/c141e5c28d3c7b532bc5792444b68aed9baa7583d90b80a9def683fee3eaeebb" :answer/id #uuid "b50a1caa-b927-5265-a694-1bdf2b5ddaa7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86619/selection/b861b0bcf52af01d49ca4d053ed43711c3065ef0897db710f47211e3c3686f50" :answer/id #uuid "e8a925bb-9cd0-5b16-8b7e-ad218de9f1e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,3\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86619/selection/9062376caa2795df71db944e32edb0fdacca33d5ddca9f791d394aa2bc7466d6" :answer/id #uuid "45540c78-9e69-5f00-95fb-ed2690378cfe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86619/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4df8eef-e36f-5a3e-ab1b-7f3cc68d497f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-86619/selection/c141e5c28d3c7b532bc5792444b68aed9baa7583d90b80a9def683fee3eaeebb" :answer-field/choices ["q-86619/selection/9b81f8a8be5c85a0e797ee8baaeba21f0dc31b57428fc18c3dd70922899b39be" "q-86619/selection/ec482aa227fa390257d545d04f1877e92823b4d16cc98918069db0c4b0f1da14" "q-86619/selection/c141e5c28d3c7b532bc5792444b68aed9baa7583d90b80a9def683fee3eaeebb" "q-86619/selection/b861b0bcf52af01d49ca4d053ed43711c3065ef0897db710f47211e3c3686f50" "q-86619/selection/9062376caa2795df71db944e32edb0fdacca33d5ddca9f791d394aa2bc7466d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-86619" :question/id #uuid "dbdb76b1-154e-56f4-8c53-1a3f3061c333" :question/math-academy-id "q-86619" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$  Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(3\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+18} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(\\frac{6}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=(3,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-86619/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-86619"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-40826/selection/1354e29f51a69561b68d4c59a6fd705e4bd619659e42c397fea5a64262c87194" :answer/id #uuid "4325b6f4-81a3-52d0-9d82-c6657defe646" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40826/selection/5a3f2e868891107902bb9a5b4b97daa73383660254dd16449ffd397917d303a0" :answer/id #uuid "1074de6c-fc5b-5bc2-b180-1d1afa758926" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40826/selection/855043f0b1ff46d54498d12b117f62a486e3b561845c8755d5681196c85234da" :answer/id #uuid "20130431-0d2d-531e-b8c8-ee3f7bbcff6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{3},8\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40826/selection/57d971cf3b7ed963cd05f3e63f8bf3c84cc7310851b580f40644d1f48d55db73" :answer/id #uuid "c582e450-fd13-5f62-8885-2162059c2da3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},4\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40826/selection/21b8af9a5d3c156c55eae04d5aeccd0a35da9f7b7bff22a948f63c6a52fd845e" :answer/id #uuid "7da24958-c102-5419-802f-6adc6f1cd558" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{2},8\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40826/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a8de227-8472-5c81-ab7b-830ec478a832" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-40826/selection/1354e29f51a69561b68d4c59a6fd705e4bd619659e42c397fea5a64262c87194" :answer-field/choices ["q-40826/selection/1354e29f51a69561b68d4c59a6fd705e4bd619659e42c397fea5a64262c87194" "q-40826/selection/5a3f2e868891107902bb9a5b4b97daa73383660254dd16449ffd397917d303a0" "q-40826/selection/855043f0b1ff46d54498d12b117f62a486e3b561845c8755d5681196c85234da" "q-40826/selection/57d971cf3b7ed963cd05f3e63f8bf3c84cc7310851b580f40644d1f48d55db73" "q-40826/selection/21b8af9a5d3c156c55eae04d5aeccd0a35da9f7b7bff22a948f63c6a52fd845e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-40826" :question/id #uuid "70ccad16-095b-5f4c-aa4b-ada6d051e6fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-40826" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(8,\\theta +\\pi ).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(8,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+\\pi )=(8,\\frac{9\\pi }{4})=(8,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-40826/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-40826"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-276838/field-1/f169f92c1946b4a86dcd40a5cae1a30ea50a84a49ecc59038782fea0bf2058cc" :answer/id #uuid "5c40f843-0631-58b4-a06f-6194eb70da85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276838/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8a9ad845-ec06-5c93-a188-15ae28b31b4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-276838/field-1/f169f92c1946b4a86dcd40a5cae1a30ea50a84a49ecc59038782fea0bf2058cc" :answer-field/choices ["q-276838/field-1/f169f92c1946b4a86dcd40a5cae1a30ea50a84a49ecc59038782fea0bf2058cc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-276838/field-2/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer/id #uuid "b8753972-949b-559c-ae7f-2d99a21abb54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276838/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "13d93c35-a9f4-5a65-afe7-4b3454045b6d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-276838/field-2/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer-field/choices ["q-276838/field-2/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-276838" :question/id #uuid "f9180b14-eb26-561a-9fe7-e0f704e71580" :question/math-academy-id "q-276838" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(5,0).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{0}{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the first quadrant, we have $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}=0.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(5,0+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(5,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-276838/field-1" "q-276838/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-276838"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120468/selection/bd38bfeb9bfc673ffe6d76eaface75e9af2f65fcceb3ce6b0329c0c6d5123d6a" :answer/id #uuid "b57ebf74-1109-5e2c-aced-3cda5c4c6308" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120468/selection/c7d7f5865a04f287adfbc7ccac92458329d02c053e1fa27cc0df187ff8f49c48" :answer/id #uuid "66c4b605-0aca-51e3-b8fe-e6c77be3a459" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120468/selection/3f3f0f644cdd72ae8506cc1dbf2d5df7f9f151c287427266fa47c30af51bc89d" :answer/id #uuid "b0599bbf-0a2a-5bf6-acdd-acbe5d243900" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120468/selection/e03b06bf248cd662e61b3dea2ac75da56aa7449dc8bb6e4b62f259b0801107e1" :answer/id #uuid "98ab9bcc-c3cf-50a5-8d34-6919868f579e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120468/selection/4243e6462c01a0f8c7929e248821c0cb16d103501f063bc34b2e3fe7fa55bb10" :answer/id #uuid "bc77e445-0135-5876-a0a7-dec8d36ff865" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120468/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2def96d6-5ad6-5ae8-a97f-084f0839a124" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120468/selection/c7d7f5865a04f287adfbc7ccac92458329d02c053e1fa27cc0df187ff8f49c48" :answer-field/choices ["q-120468/selection/bd38bfeb9bfc673ffe6d76eaface75e9af2f65fcceb3ce6b0329c0c6d5123d6a" "q-120468/selection/c7d7f5865a04f287adfbc7ccac92458329d02c053e1fa27cc0df187ff8f49c48" "q-120468/selection/3f3f0f644cdd72ae8506cc1dbf2d5df7f9f151c287427266fa47c30af51bc89d" "q-120468/selection/e03b06bf248cd662e61b3dea2ac75da56aa7449dc8bb6e4b62f259b0801107e1" "q-120468/selection/4243e6462c01a0f8c7929e248821c0cb16d103501f063bc34b2e3fe7fa55bb10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120468" :question/id #uuid "256dfcfa-6ea4-5478-bf71-0a81ef7db2c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-120468" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1/\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=(4,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,-4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-120468/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120468"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-276803/field-1/e23f9ea338f175b3f5a7621b94518bec0d179d6cb526e41f6e659cfd147c4d14" :answer/id #uuid "dcd50afe-3eb7-5386-b4d6-52582e3a30cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276803/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c14cda7-3017-549b-a975-ccfa2bad06de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-276803/field-1/e23f9ea338f175b3f5a7621b94518bec0d179d6cb526e41f6e659cfd147c4d14" :answer-field/choices ["q-276803/field-1/e23f9ea338f175b3f5a7621b94518bec0d179d6cb526e41f6e659cfd147c4d14"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-276803/field-2/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "0eebaf68-1aa6-5758-a417-bf5386698813" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276803/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9310a9-02c3-566b-aee6-6d2953623dfd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-276803/field-2/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer-field/choices ["q-276803/field-2/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-276803" :question/id #uuid "188fe0f5-6988-52fa-91db-08219b61d9bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-276803" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{3},1).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{1}{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-276803/field-1" "q-276803/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-276803"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120595/selection/3f3f0f644cdd72ae8506cc1dbf2d5df7f9f151c287427266fa47c30af51bc89d" :answer/id #uuid "b5a250a8-3dfa-5f81-8455-561a61b5b21a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120595/selection/811fe92f393cfa21932278991448fe1e8fbb07e9d882b2dd974c59b52778007d" :answer/id #uuid "993e94ea-20f3-5b92-bfb2-0985049bca80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120595/selection/2395f90f05e12ce2c50e1314814956063c82a117996ca6cca13f6d3dda895bb1" :answer/id #uuid "0713e35e-6435-5ac5-b404-deb8a7f6b610" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120595/selection/d3f5a886eabf1ddf47d6d1affb57d24049b25510db016cecc348459bcf29346c" :answer/id #uuid "2b957cb0-8603-5450-8ba2-baad7c038789" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{3},2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120595/selection/e8b6e48d2640b190477b067d73ed38c8ae23d6a2118701d65bc1f8aac439d661" :answer/id #uuid "777a4b0d-d347-581d-879a-af2dca7cbe74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120595/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "aee33c74-a634-5edd-9349-06ba0d8bbd43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120595/selection/2395f90f05e12ce2c50e1314814956063c82a117996ca6cca13f6d3dda895bb1" :answer-field/choices ["q-120595/selection/3f3f0f644cdd72ae8506cc1dbf2d5df7f9f151c287427266fa47c30af51bc89d" "q-120595/selection/811fe92f393cfa21932278991448fe1e8fbb07e9d882b2dd974c59b52778007d" "q-120595/selection/2395f90f05e12ce2c50e1314814956063c82a117996ca6cca13f6d3dda895bb1" "q-120595/selection/d3f5a886eabf1ddf47d6d1affb57d24049b25510db016cecc348459bcf29346c" "q-120595/selection/e8b6e48d2640b190477b067d73ed38c8ae23d6a2118701d65bc1f8aac439d661"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120595" :question/id #uuid "de8834e5-df0a-5c36-914b-a1ed971ccd20" :question/math-academy-id "q-120595" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952748/assets/5ff0219f3f76441dbffc770ce0b8f2481cca8c46b8aa832ab7893d472f3026ae.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=4.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4,\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-2,2\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-120595/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120595"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81224/selection/6d708f79cbae82cf5ef144ea3c4363f54afee3bf8f69d04f960b306082b2340c" :answer/id #uuid "9614c3f1-7370-5131-b1ed-819663b373c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-6,-4\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81224/selection/c8c54565801b62bab0807a51a61a6ab31bdfd2a66e93772a61757011a2c7de4b" :answer/id #uuid "96b84ae8-5a62-5979-9651-67e8955deacc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81224/selection/5e7d9edaa18344df84b15d592164aad2921d7be74e938887474ef6af474f06dd" :answer/id #uuid "343c0eb9-7476-530e-98c3-341f3b5dddb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-5,-4\\sqrt{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81224/selection/3ea3fd2ef3abf89d892aec9fddf5649bb61a9f2ab821765e1f7ece9905f75bbb" :answer/id #uuid "25792196-76aa-5410-907f-17e0ce4f433b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81224/selection/34d83ba0dd8eb04cf6656636bdea862c8cd0df5e083f3d0eb130321e38ffb479" :answer/id #uuid "f8fd58fe-4896-5fe7-aad7-91faa4f78dbc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{11}{2},-4\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81224/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "04433b50-8f27-51fb-aae8-a5df454ceded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81224/selection/3ea3fd2ef3abf89d892aec9fddf5649bb61a9f2ab821765e1f7ece9905f75bbb" :answer-field/choices ["q-81224/selection/6d708f79cbae82cf5ef144ea3c4363f54afee3bf8f69d04f960b306082b2340c" "q-81224/selection/c8c54565801b62bab0807a51a61a6ab31bdfd2a66e93772a61757011a2c7de4b" "q-81224/selection/5e7d9edaa18344df84b15d592164aad2921d7be74e938887474ef6af474f06dd" "q-81224/selection/3ea3fd2ef3abf89d892aec9fddf5649bb61a9f2ab821765e1f7ece9905f75bbb" "q-81224/selection/34d83ba0dd8eb04cf6656636bdea862c8cd0df5e083f3d0eb130321e38ffb479"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81224" :question/id #uuid "eeed4d5b-d5f2-546d-acc5-ae37a625911e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81224" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952748/assets/a615f7a80b81f89fccd8c82bf65d65e3a4a65958ec8a6fbc746ae68749949a87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ and $m∠AOB=\\pi ,$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we must have $r=10.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $10,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $10.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(10,\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-81224/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81224"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-277012/field-1/05402db46107d70ece210f0bd5e24b332ae4f43bd474d592035c3e3c92625629" :answer/id #uuid "c59c4311-7e00-5f7f-8733-9fa2f55748aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-277012/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f71cd2d-b7fb-5953-aada-7492f4f7add2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-277012/field-1/05402db46107d70ece210f0bd5e24b332ae4f43bd474d592035c3e3c92625629" :answer-field/choices ["q-277012/field-1/05402db46107d70ece210f0bd5e24b332ae4f43bd474d592035c3e3c92625629"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-277012/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "967cbe30-b750-5581-a87f-808a1ee4c15d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-277012/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f039044-546d-51dc-a1c5-66b80a481b23" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-277012/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-277012/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-277012" :question/id #uuid "a83f5e13-021d-5699-bc54-d8ad3c050b9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-277012" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952748/assets/d931b377a8d62bf3b56672a0f556c0581e8400c5b1c7b1f783f08e4297342855.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4,{240}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={210}^{∘}.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4,{240}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=4.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${240}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={240}^{∘}-{210}^{∘}={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4,{30}^{∘})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-277012/field-1" "q-277012/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-277012"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120597/selection/a8788fd8e34948fc62a48231115f6871c4669aeea6791478c777440a3acb8fd7" :answer/id #uuid "e2b14ad3-33d1-50ce-9cd6-5969e82aba55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120597/selection/26b9762d1db9d4a698e28127322bead3f7278149ff53be860a2cc34266ce8f9a" :answer/id #uuid "153bf9d5-5ec5-55ff-96e2-6a413e26cff2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,-2\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120597/selection/06468055e014e3f866f2abe895b358d7cc983bea6cbfbee10b94f5a5b2ea1f12" :answer/id #uuid "59089e7d-733a-5a7d-9851-d20e38c5e458" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{2},-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120597/selection/7090357a881be4d33620bf847a636d29bd7e0bf68deea0064d5d0efe5519ab88" :answer/id #uuid "0e2ab7a2-ece3-55d4-a636-667e4ec5fcf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120597/selection/c9181665f417390f9888c44c8ef90bd7863c98df00f661f12953701a829e421b" :answer/id #uuid "d9ce342e-755b-590d-8890-1181d0837260" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,4\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120597/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce045a4f-b2e7-5441-89c8-84712af99447" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120597/selection/a8788fd8e34948fc62a48231115f6871c4669aeea6791478c777440a3acb8fd7" :answer-field/choices ["q-120597/selection/a8788fd8e34948fc62a48231115f6871c4669aeea6791478c777440a3acb8fd7" "q-120597/selection/26b9762d1db9d4a698e28127322bead3f7278149ff53be860a2cc34266ce8f9a" "q-120597/selection/06468055e014e3f866f2abe895b358d7cc983bea6cbfbee10b94f5a5b2ea1f12" "q-120597/selection/7090357a881be4d33620bf847a636d29bd7e0bf68deea0064d5d0efe5519ab88" "q-120597/selection/c9181665f417390f9888c44c8ef90bd7863c98df00f661f12953701a829e421b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120597" :question/id #uuid "ae9fb0df-51c8-5805-8a9a-3fb0f6d09879" :question/math-academy-id "q-120597" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952748/assets/fd38e4bed4f11f07f2c6901a74c7543655f5ff8a95b8b049e54ff4ff4dd409ee.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={165}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=4\\sqrt{2}.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4\\sqrt{2},$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4\\sqrt{2}.$  \n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${150}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={150}^{∘}+{165}^{∘}={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4,-4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-120597/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120597"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-277027/field-1/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "caf700d8-e674-5c42-bdca-25ab22c82965" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-277027/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2e6b5ca2-baf7-510e-8c4f-1ee7f6e5f63b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-277027/field-1/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer-field/choices ["q-277027/field-1/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-277027/field-2/b336fe0bb5249057b2ff0ec170d4f4011a59ade6c56e9a4ab5b5763d0144b9f6" :answer/id #uuid "3a403334-4552-5733-b103-9125811367ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-277027/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "3b14c52f-ef19-5ae5-bb78-f8a0e01ae8dd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-277027/field-2/b336fe0bb5249057b2ff0ec170d4f4011a59ade6c56e9a4ab5b5763d0144b9f6" :answer-field/choices ["q-277027/field-2/b336fe0bb5249057b2ff0ec170d4f4011a59ade6c56e9a4ab5b5763d0144b9f6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-277027" :question/id #uuid "01215fdb-7672-5686-a3c5-4762cb00c7f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-277027" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952748/assets/dbca0b9bd96eb68b7f874df74848bb887037a7de2ed7613bc848c40f78feff4f.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(10,{60}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={60}^{∘}.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(10,{60}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=10.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $10,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $10.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counter-clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${60}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={60}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}={120}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(10,{120}^{∘})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,5\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-277027/field-1" "q-277027/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-277027"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
